    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense about the rye. I've noticed that even when I keep the temp steady, I'll get these random days where the starter just seems... chaotic. Like, one day it's perfectly predictable and the next it's acting totally erratic or \"off\" for no apparent reason. Is there a way to track if certain microbes are outcompeting others during these spikes, or is it just a matter of environmental control?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Tracking specific microbial competition requires advanced lab work, but you can monitor trends using regular pH and activity logging. Those unpredictable spikes often indicate that a dominant, fast-acting culture is temporarily over-reproducing before another balances it out. To mitigate this \"chaos,\" try slightly increasing the feeding ratio to dilute the existing culture more heavily during peak instability. This regular resetting helps prevent any single dominant strain from taking over too aggressively.",
